  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows high amplitude troughing across the\nwestern CONUS, with relatively high amplitude mid/upper ridging\ndownstream across the central CONUS. At the surface, the region\nremains on the western edge of a ~1035 mb surface high currently\ncentered over central Appalachia. This has resulted in pleasant\nconditions across the CWA this afternoon, with temperatures\naround 60 degrees (around 10 degrees above seasonal normals for\ntoday), clear skies, and 10 mph southerly winds. Generally clear\nskies and light winds tonight will promote radiational cooling \ntonight, but low temperatures for Wednesday morning should\nremain above freezing CWA wide.\n\nAs we head into Wednesday afternoon, the aforementioned western\nupper trough and accompanying surface cyclone is progged to \nmove into the Northern Plains. This will likely bring in some\nscattered high level cloud cover, as well as increasing \nsoutherly winds to 10 to 15 mph with gusts up to 20-25 mph.\nHigh temperatures tomorrow should be a few degrees warmer\ncompared to today, as well, with highs in the lower 60s and even\nwarmer overnight lows for Wednesday night/Thursday morning (low\nto mid 40s). The upper trough is anticipated to move across the\nUpper Midwest and eventually into Ontario by Thursday afternoon,\nwith the attendant cold front remaining well north of the\nregion, with west southwesterly mid level flow remaining in\nplace over central Illinois. This will allow for the gradual\nwarming trend to continue, with afternoon highs in the mid 60s\non Thursday and perhaps as warm as the upper 60s on Friday; and \nwill also promote decent moisture return, with dewpoints \nincreasing into the mid to upper 40s. \n\nA stronger surface low and upper wave is expected to emerge from\nthe central Rockies and traverse into the Upper Midwest on\nSaturday, helping send a cold front through the region Saturday\nnight. Precipitation chances look to begin west of I-55 \nSaturday afternoon, and linger east of I-57 into Sunday morning,\nwith 30-70 percent chances for precipitation across the area. \nLight precipitation amounts are expected for most areas with \nunder a quarter inch expected, however a few thunderstorms are \npossible with locally heavier precipitation amounts. Ensemble \nforecast system depicts 30 percent joint probability of 30+ kts \ndeep-layer shear with 100+ J/kg MUCAPE, so will need to keep \nmonitoring for the need to include thunderstorms in the forecast\ngrids as well as severe thunderstorm potential should these \nparameters significantly exceed these thresholds (less than 15% \nchance).\n\nMuch colder air and dry conditions will arrive following Saturday \nnight's cold front, with highs likely in the 50s and blustery \nSunday, followed by 40s Monday and Tuesday and lows dropping below \nfreezing again.",
